Package: aurapass
Version: @VERSION@
Section: utils
Priority: optional
Architecture: @ARCH@
Depends: libc6 (>= @LIBC@), libgcc-s1, libx11-6, libxkbcommon0, libwayland-client0
Recommends: xdg-utils, zenity, fonts-dejavu-core, gnome-shell-extension-appindicator
Installed-Size: @SIZE@
Maintainer: pa1 <pa1@aurapass.invalid>
Homepage: https://github.com/aurapass/aurapass
Description: offline-first password manager
 AuraPass keeps passwords, one-time codes, notes and other secrets in a
 single encrypted vault on your own computer. Nothing leaves the machine:
 the vault is a local file and the database is local. Chromium talks to
 the app through a loopback bridge; Firefox uses the browser's native
 messaging channel, so it is not affected by HTTPS-Only or local-network
 restrictions.
 .
 The vault is protected with Argon2id (a memory-hard key derivation
 function) and XChaCha20-Poly1305 authenticated encryption, in a
 documented, versioned container format.
 .
 On Wayland desktops the app runs through XWayland, because the Wayland
 protocol lets a client neither hide its window into the tray nor choose
 where a pop-up appears.
 .
 The desktop application draws its interface in software, so it needs no
 GPU, no EGL and no OpenGL driver; it starts and idles in a few tens of
 megabytes. gnome-shell-extension-appindicator is recommended so the tray
 icon shows on GNOME; zenity is recommended so Open/Save use the desktop's
 native file chooser.
 .
 This package installs:
  * aurapass-desktop     - the graphical application,
  * aurapass             - the command-line tool,
  * aurapass-bridge      - the localhost bridge for the browser extensions,
  * aurapass-native-host - the Firefox native-messaging relay.
 .
 Browser extensions (Chromium and Firefox) are installed, unpacked, under
 /usr/share/aurapass/extensions/.
